  793                                   ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  794                                   ; The effect machinery. The per-frame stage lives inlined in YX6_play; what
  795                                   ; follows is everything it drives: the two slot descriptors, the tick
  796                                   ; handlers, and the one slot machine that serves both timers.
  797                                   ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  798                                   
  799                                   ; The slot descriptors - see the SD_ equates. Slot 1 owns Timer A, slot 2
  800                                   ; Timer D, whose control nibble is shared with Timer C and only ever changed
  801                                   ; read-modify-write; the slot machine uses that form for both, which on
  802                                   ; Timer A writes the same values a clear would.
  803                                           even
  804                                   yx6_desc1:
  805  000004C4  0000                           dc.b    0,0                     ; E last, T last
  806  000004C6  FA19FA1F0134                   dc.w    $FA19,$FA1F,$0134       ; control, data, vector
  807  000004CC  00000000                       dc.l    0                       ; PARM, set per effect
  808  000004D0  0000000000000000               dc.l    0,0                     ; ISR block, other slot: yx6_link
  809                                   yx6_desc2:
  810  000004D8  0000                           dc.b    0,0
  811  000004DA  FA1DFA250110                   dc.w    $FA1D,$FA25,$0110
  812  000004E0  00000000                       dc.l    0
  813  000004E4  0000000000000000               dc.l    0,0
  814                                   
  815                                   ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  816                                   ; The tick handlers, in maxYMiser's shipping forms: the PSG select and write
  817                                   ; in as few instructions as the bus allows, patched immediates instead of
  818                                   ; state, software end-of-interrupt as TOS runs the MFP - written as an
  819                                   ; immediate byte, since the in-service registers ignore written ones. All
  820                                   ; MFP interrupts arrive at 68000 level 6, so these never nest, and every
  821                                   ; patched operand is addressed pc-relatively so the player stays loadable
  822                                   ; anywhere - which also means it must run from RAM, where a demo lives.
  823                                   ;
  824                                   ; The two slots' handlers are the same text twice, and MUST stay
  825                                   ; byte-congruent: the slot machine and yx6_drum_start reach every patched
  826                                   ; operand through ISR_ offsets measured on the A block. The rig checks the
  827                                   ; congruence from the symbol table. The park entries - where YX6_init aims
  828                                   ; the vectors while no effect runs - are the handlers' own tails.
  829                                   ;
  830                                   ; The timers STACK: each handler drops the IPL to 5 as soon as its PSG
  831                                   ; select/data pair is complete, so the MFP's higher-priority channel -
  832                                   ; Timer A over Timer D - can nest instead of waiting a whole tick. The
  833                                   ; nesting is strictly one-way: Timer A interrupts Timer D's handlers,
  834                                   ; never the reverse (SEI mode admits only higher MFP priorities while an
  835                                   ; in-service bit is set), and nothing of the player's sits above Timer A.
  836                                   ; That direction is why the USP park belongs to the D-side drum alone: the
  837                                   ; A-side drum - the one that can nest into a parked window - always uses
  838                                   ; the stack, so a parked USP survives. The drop must never come before
  839                                   ; the handler's LAST chip write: a nested tick re-selects the PSG.
  840                                   ;
  841                                   ; The patch offsets are load-bearing:
  842                                   ;   drum:  select immediate at +3, sample address at +8, flag mask at _clear+3
  843                                   ;   sid:   select at +2 and volume at +4 inside the imm long; the partner's
  844                                   ;          address low word at +14, past the IPL drop, patched by yx6_link
  845                                   ;   buzz:  the shape at +4
  846                                   ; The SID vector swap patches the vector's LOW word only, so the two halves
  847                                   ; of a block must share their address high word: keep the player inside one
  848                                   ; 64K segment - anywhere a program smaller than 64K naturally loads.
  849                                   ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  850                                   
  851                                   ; One drum tick: the sample byte goes straight to the voice's volume register.
  852                                   ; The byte after the last has bit 7 set, so the same move answers "done": park
  853                                   ; the volume mid-scale against the click, stop the timer, drop the flag.
  854                                   yx6_drumA:
  855  000004EC  11FC00088800                   move.b  #8,$FFFF8800.w          ; SMC +3: select the drummed volume
  856  000004F2  11F9123456788802               move.b  $12345678,$FFFF8802.w   ; SMC +8: the next sample byte
  857  000004FA  6Bxx                           bmi.s   .ended
  858  000004FC  46FC2500                       move.w  #$2500,sr               ; pair done: the other timer may stack
  859  00000500  2F08                           move.l  a0,-(sp)                ; the stack, never the USP: this side
  860  00000502  41FAFFF0                       lea     yx6_drumA+8(pc),a0      ; nests into parked windows
  861  00000506  5290                           addq.l  #1,(a0)                 ; step the operand itself
  862  00000508  205F                           movea.l (sp)+,a0
  863  0000050A  11FC00DFFA0F                   move.b  #$DF,$FFFFFA0F.w
  864  00000510  4E73                           rte
  865                                   .ended:
  866  00000512  11FC000D8802                   move.b  #$D,$FFFF8802.w         ; middle volume: no click
  867  00000518  023800F0FA19                   andi.b  #$F0,$FFFFFA19.w        ; the sample ran out: stop Timer A
  868  0000051E  46FC2500                       move.w  #$2500,sr
  869  00000522  2F08                           move.l  a0,-(sp)
  870  00000524  41FAxxxx                       lea     yx6_drums(pc),a0
  871                                   yx6_drumA_clear:
  872  00000528  021000FF                       andi.b  #$FF,(a0)               ; SMC +3: drop the voice's drum flag
  873  0000052C  205F                           movea.l (sp)+,a0
  874                                   yx6_parkA:
  875  0000052E  11FC00DFFA0F                   move.b  #$DF,$FFFFFA0F.w
  876  00000534  4E73                           rte
  877                                   
  878                                   ; The SID square: each tick writes one half of the square - the volume or
  879                                   ; silence - and installs the other half as the next tick, one word into the
  880                                   ; vector. Select and data land in one move.l: the immediate's bytes hit
  881                                   ; $8800 (select) and $8802 (data) by bus layout.
  882                                   yx6_sidA_on:
  883  00000536  21FC08000F008800               move.l  #$08000F00,$FFFF8800.w  ; SMC +2 select, +4 volume
  884  0000053E  46FC2500                       move.w  #$2500,sr
  885  00000542  31FC00000136                   move.w  #0,$136.w               ; SMC +14: the quiet half is next
  886  00000548  11FC00DFFA0F                   move.b  #$DF,$FFFFFA0F.w
  887  0000054E  4E73                           rte
  888                                   yx6_sidA_off:
  889  00000550  21FC080000008800               move.l  #$08000000,$FFFF8800.w  ; SMC +2 select; silence
  890  00000558  46FC2500                       move.w  #$2500,sr
  891  0000055C  31FC00000136                   move.w  #0,$136.w               ; SMC +14: the loud half is next
  892  00000562  11FC00DFFA0F                   move.b  #$DF,$FFFFFA0F.w
  893  00000568  4E73                           rte
  894                                   
  895                                   ; The sync-buzzer tick: writing the shape to R13 restarts the envelope, and
  896                                   ; that restart at the timer's rate is the whole effect.
  897                                   yx6_buzzA:
  898  0000056A  21FC0D0000008800               move.l  #$0D000000,$FFFF8800.w  ; SMC +4: the shape
  899  00000572  46FC2500                       move.w  #$2500,sr
  900  00000576  11FC00DFFA0F                   move.b  #$DF,$FFFFFA0F.w
  901  0000057C  4E73                           rte
  902                                   
  903                                   ; Slot 2's instances: Timer D's stop, vector and in-service bit, otherwise
  904                                   ; the A block byte for byte.
  905                                   yx6_drumD:
  906  0000057E  11FC00088800                   move.b  #8,$FFFF8800.w
  907  00000584  11F9123456788802               move.b  $12345678,$FFFF8802.w
  908  0000058C  6Bxx                           bmi.s   .ended
  909  0000058E  46FC2500                       move.w  #$2500,sr               ; Timer A may nest from here on, and
  910                                           .if YX6_SUPER_HOST              ; never parks the USP itself - so
  911                                 -         move    a0,usp                  ; this side may
  912                                 -         .else
  913  00000592  2F08                           move.l  a0,-(sp)
  914                                           .endif
  915  00000594  41FAFFF0                       lea     yx6_drumD+8(pc),a0
  916  00000598  5290                           addq.l  #1,(a0)
  917                                           .if YX6_SUPER_HOST
  918                                 -         move    usp,a0
  919                                 -         .else
  920  0000059A  205F                           movea.l (sp)+,a0
  921                                           .endif
  922  0000059C  11FC00EFFA11                   move.b  #$EF,$FFFFFA11.w
  923  000005A2  4E73                           rte
  924                                   .ended:
  925  000005A4  11FC000D8802                   move.b  #$D,$FFFF8802.w
  926  000005AA  023800F0FA1D                   andi.b  #$F0,$FFFFFA1D.w
  927  000005B0  46FC2500                       move.w  #$2500,sr
  928                                           .if YX6_SUPER_HOST
  929                                 -         move    a0,usp
  930                                 -         .else
  931  000005B4  2F08                           move.l  a0,-(sp)
  932                                           .endif
  933  000005B6  41FAxxxx                       lea     yx6_drums(pc),a0
  934                                   yx6_drumD_clear:
  935  000005BA  021000FF                       andi.b  #$FF,(a0)
  936                                           .if YX6_SUPER_HOST
  937                                 -         move    usp,a0
  938                                 -         .else
  939  000005BE  205F                           movea.l (sp)+,a0
  940                                           .endif
  941                                   yx6_parkD:
  942  000005C0  11FC00EFFA11                   move.b  #$EF,$FFFFFA11.w
  943  000005C6  4E73                           rte
  944                                   
  945                                   yx6_sidD_on:
  946  000005C8  21FC08000F008800               move.l  #$08000F00,$FFFF8800.w
  947  000005D0  46FC2500                       move.w  #$2500,sr
  948  000005D4  31FC00000112                   move.w  #0,$112.w
  949  000005DA  11FC00EFFA11                   move.b  #$EF,$FFFFFA11.w
  950  000005E0  4E73                           rte
  951                                   yx6_sidD_off:
  952  000005E2  21FC080000008800               move.l  #$08000000,$FFFF8800.w
  953  000005EA  46FC2500                       move.w  #$2500,sr
  954  000005EE  31FC00000112                   move.w  #0,$112.w
  955  000005F4  11FC00EFFA11                   move.b  #$EF,$FFFFFA11.w
  956  000005FA  4E73                           rte
  957                                   
  958                                   yx6_buzzD:
  959  000005FC  21FC0D0000008800               move.l  #$0D000000,$FFFF8800.w
  960  00000604  46FC2500                       move.w  #$2500,sr
  961  00000608  11FC00EFFA11                   move.b  #$EF,$FFFFFA11.w
  962  0000060E  4E73                           rte
  963                                   
  964                                   yx6_drums:
  965  00000610  00                             dc.b    0                       ; voice bits 0-2: a drum is running
  966  00000611  00                             even
  967                                   
  968                                   ; Where the patched operands sit, measured on the A block; the D block is
  969                                   ; byte-congruent, so the same offsets serve both through SD_ISR.
  970                                   ; rmac evaluates left to right: the parentheses are load-bearing.
  971           =00000003               ISR_DRUM_SEL    equ     3
  972           =00000008               ISR_DRUM_PTR    equ     8
  973           =0000003F               ISR_CLEAR       equ     (yx6_drumA_clear-yx6_drumA)+3
  974           =0000004A               ISR_SIDON       equ     yx6_sidA_on-yx6_drumA
  975           =0000004C               ISR_SIDON_SEL   equ     ISR_SIDON+2
  976           =0000004E               ISR_SID_VOL     equ     ISR_SIDON+4
  977           =00000058               ISR_SIDON_NEXT  equ     ISR_SIDON+14
  978           =00000064               ISR_SIDOFF      equ     yx6_sidA_off-yx6_drumA
  979           =00000066               ISR_SIDOFF_SEL  equ     ISR_SIDOFF+2
  980           =00000072               ISR_SIDOFF_NEXT equ     ISR_SIDOFF+14
  981           =0000007E               ISR_BUZZ        equ     yx6_buzzA-yx6_drumA
  982           =00000082               ISR_BUZZ_SHAPE  equ     ISR_BUZZ+4
  983                                   
  984                                   ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  985                                   ; yx6_link, from YX6_init: the run-time addresses only a loaded player knows.
  986                                   ; Each descriptor gets its ISR block and its partner, both slots' last code
  987                                   ; and count are cleared, and the SID halves get each other's address low
  988                                   ; word - the vector's high word never changes, and neither do these, so
  989                                   ; they are patched once here rather than on every SID start.
  990                                   ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  991                                   yx6_link:
  992  00000612  7402                           moveq   #2,d2                   ; every burst gate open: a previous
  993  00000614  3F02                   .gates: move.w  d2,-(sp)                ; session's mute must not leak
  994  00000616  7802                           moveq   #2,d4
  995  00000618  6100xxxx                       bsr     yx6_burst_gate
  996  0000061C  341F                           move.w  (sp)+,d2
  997  0000061E  51CAFFF4                       dbra    d2,.gates
  998  00000622  41FAFEA0                       lea     yx6_desc1(pc),a0
  999  00000626  43FAFEB0                       lea     yx6_desc2(pc),a1
 1000  0000062A  21490010                       move.l  a1,SD_OTHER(a0)
 1001  0000062E  23480010                       move.l  a0,SD_OTHER(a1)
 1002  00000632  4250                           clr.w   (a0)                    ; E and T last, both slots
 1003  00000634  4251                           clr.w   (a1)
 1004  00000636  45FAFEB4                       lea     yx6_drumA(pc),a2
 1005  0000063A  214A000C                       move.l  a2,SD_ISR(a0)
 1006  0000063E  61xx                           bsr.s   .partners
 1007  00000640  45FAFF3C                       lea     yx6_drumD(pc),a2
 1008  00000644  234A000C                       move.l  a2,SD_ISR(a1)
 1009                                   .partners:
 1010  00000648  41EA0064                       lea     ISR_SIDOFF(a2),a0       ; each half installs the other
 1011  0000064C  2008                           move.l  a0,d0
 1012  0000064E  35400058                       move.w  d0,ISR_SIDON_NEXT(a2)
 1013  00000652  41EA004A                       lea     ISR_SIDON(a2),a0
 1014  00000656  2008                           move.l  a0,d0
 1015  00000658  35400072                       move.w  d0,ISR_SIDOFF_NEXT(a2)
 1016  0000065C  4E75                           rts
 1017                                   
 1018                                   ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1019                                   ; yx6_slot: one slot of the effect stage - both slots, through a5.
 1020                                   ; A changed code reprograms the slot's timer from scratch, a held one
 1021                                   ; updates only the count (a running MFP timer loads it at its next reload),
 1022                                   ; a dropped one stops the timer - unless the slot ran a drum, which
 1023                                   ; finishes by itself. A held DRUM code is a trigger on EVERY frame it is
 1024                                   ; present, as the reference player starts one on each coded frame: real
 1025                                   ; dumps carry back-to-back codes whose ring bytes name different samples -
 1026                                   ; an attack, then a body - and only a full restart plays the second one.
 1027                                   ;
 1028                                   ; in: d0.b = E (never 0 with E-last also 0: the caller's gate), d1.b = T,
 1029                                   ;     a5 = the slot's descriptor, a3 = workspace, d3.l = N.
 1030                                   ; preserves d3/a3/a5; clobbers d0-d2/d4-d5, a0-a2/a4.
 1031                                   ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1032                                   yx6_slot:
 1033  0000065E  B015                           cmp.b   (a5),d0
 1034  00000660  6700xxxx                       beq.w   .held
 1035  00000664  1815                           move.b  (a5),d4                 ; what the slot was doing before
 1036  00000666  1A80                           move.b  d0,(a5)
 1037  00000668  4A00                           tst.b   d0
 1038  0000066A  6700xxxx                       beq.w   .released
 1039                                   ; A new code: find the parameter register's ring - r8+voice carries the SID
 1040                                   ; volume, the drum number or the buzzer shape - then let the type arm its
 1041                                   ; handler; every type then (re)programs the timer the same way. The voice
 1042                                   ; stays live in d5, the parameter byte in a1, for whoever needs them.
 1043  0000066E  1A00                           move.b  d0,d5
 1044  00000670  E80D                           lsr.b   #4,d5
 1045  00000672  CA7C0003                       and.w   #3,d5
 1046  00000676  5345                           subq.w  #1,d5                   ; the voice, 0..2
 1047  00000678  3405                           move.w  d5,d2
 1048  0000067A  5042                           addq.w  #8,d2
 1049  0000067C  C4EB0024                       mulu.w  YX6_N(a3),d2
 1050  00000680  2B420008                       move.l  d2,SD_PARM(a5)
 1051  00000684  2253                           movea.l YX6_CURSOR(a3),a1
 1052  00000686  D3C2                           adda.l  d2,a1                   ; a1 = the slot's parameter byte
 1053  00000688  1400                           move.b  d0,d2                   ; (d4 still holds the OLD code, for
 1054  0000068A  C43C00C0                       and.b   #$C0,d2                 ; the retune test below)
 1055  0000068E  67xx                           beq.s   .sid
 1056  00000690  B43C0040                       cmp.b   #$40,d2
 1057  00000694  67xx                           beq.s   .drum
 1058  00000696  1811                           move.b  (a1),d4                 ; $C0, the buzzer ($80 never packs):
 1059  00000698  C83C000F                       and.b   #15,d4                  ; the shape rides the tick's immediate
 1060  0000069C  206D000C                       movea.l SD_ISR(a5),a0
 1061  000006A0  11440082                       move.b  d4,ISR_BUZZ_SHAPE(a0)
 1062  000006A4  41E8007E                       lea     ISR_BUZZ(a0),a0
 1063  000006A8  60xx                           bra.s   .armed
 1064                                   .sid:
 1065                                   ; While a drum owns this voice, its volume register is the drum's alone: the
 1066                                   ; SID stays un-armed and the cleared last-code makes every following frame
 1067                                   ; retry, so it comes back by itself the frame after the drum ends. That is
 1068                                   ; the reference player's priority, where the drum overrides the volume.
 1069  000006AA  7401                           moveq   #1,d2                   ; (d4 still holds the OLD code)
 1070  000006AC  EB2A                           lsl.b   d5,d2
 1071  000006AE  C43AFF60                       and.b   yx6_drums(pc),d2
 1072  000006B2  67xx                           beq.s   .sid_free
 1073  000006B4  4215                           clr.b   (a5)
 1074  000006B6  6100xxxx                       bsr     yx6_burst_open_old      ; a replaced SID's gate must not leak
 1075  000006BA  4E75                           rts
 1076                                   .sid_free:
 1077                                   ; A code that differs from the old one ONLY in its prescaler is the same
 1078                                   ; SID sliding across a prescaler boundary - real tunes wobble across one
 1079                                   ; many times a second. The reference player free-runs the square's phase
 1080                                   ; through frequency changes, so keep the installed half: retune the timer
 1081                                   ; without touching the vector. A full restart here would reset the phase
 1082                                   ; to the loud half every flip - an audible ticking.
 1083  000006BC  1400                           move.b  d0,d2
 1084  000006BE  B902                           eor.b   d4,d2
 1085  000006C0  C43C00F0                       and.b   #$F0,d2                 ; type and voice unchanged?
 1086  000006C4  67xx                           beq.s   .sid_retune
 1087  000006C6  6100xxxx                       bsr     yx6_burst_open_old      ; a replaced SID's voice write resumes
 1088  000006CA  3405                           move.w  d5,d2                   ; ...and the new voice's is muted:
 1089  000006CC  7800                           moveq   #0,d4                   ; the square owns it now
 1090  000006CE  6100xxxx                       bsr     yx6_burst_gate
 1091  000006D2  3805                           move.w  d5,d4                   ; a fresh SID: the voice's volume
 1092  000006D4  5044                           addq.w  #8,d4                   ; register into both halves' selects
 1093  000006D6  206D000C                       movea.l SD_ISR(a5),a0
 1094  000006DA  1144004C                       move.b  d4,ISR_SIDON_SEL(a0)
 1095  000006DE  11440066                       move.b  d4,ISR_SIDOFF_SEL(a0)
 1096  000006E2  1811                           move.b  (a1),d4
 1097  000006E4  C83C000F                       and.b   #15,d4
 1098  000006E8  1144004E                       move.b  d4,ISR_SID_VOL(a0)      ; the loud half's volume
 1099  000006EC  41E8004A                       lea     ISR_SIDON(a0),a0
 1100  000006F0  60xx                           bra.s   .armed
 1101                                   .sid_retune:
 1102  000006F2  1811                           move.b  (a1),d4                 ; the volume still tracks the ring
 1103  000006F4  C83C000F                       and.b   #15,d4
 1104  000006F8  206D000C                       movea.l SD_ISR(a5),a0
 1105  000006FC  1144004E                       move.b  d4,ISR_SID_VOL(a0)
 1106  00000700  306D0006                       movea.w SD_VEC(a5),a0           ; keep whichever half is installed:
 1107  00000704  2050                           movea.l (a0),a0                 ; .armed writes the same value back
 1108  00000706  60xx                           bra.s   .armed
 1109                                   .drum:
 1110  00000708  6100xxxx                       bsr     yx6_drum_start
 1111                                   .armed:
 1112  0000070C  1B410001                       move.b  d1,SD_TLAST(a5)
 1113  00000710  326D0002                       movea.w SD_CTRL(a5),a1
 1114  00000714  346D0006                       movea.w SD_VEC(a5),a2
 1115  00000718  40E7                           move.w  sr,-(sp)
 1116  0000071A  007C0700                       ori.w   #$0700,sr
 1117  0000071E  021100F0                       andi.b  #$F0,(a1)               ; stop, then vector, count, prescaler
 1118  00000722  2488                           move.l  a0,(a2)
 1119  00000724  346D0004                       movea.w SD_DATA(a5),a2
 1120  00000728  1481                           move.b  d1,(a2)
 1121  0000072A  1A00                           move.b  d0,d5
 1122  0000072C  CA3C0007                       and.b   #7,d5
 1123  00000730  8B11                           or.b    d5,(a1)
 1124  00000732  46DF                           move.w  (sp)+,sr
 1125  00000734  4E75                           rts
 1126                                   .released:
 1127  00000736  1404                           move.b  d4,d2                   ; the level dropped: stop the timer -
 1128  00000738  C43C00C0                       and.b   #$C0,d2                 ; unless a drum is finishing by itself
 1129  0000073C  B43C0040                       cmp.b   #$40,d2
 1130  00000740  67xx                           beq.s   .out
 1131  00000742  326D0002                       movea.w SD_CTRL(a5),a1
 1132  00000746  021100F0                       andi.b  #$F0,(a1)
 1133  0000074A  4A02                           tst.b   d2                      ; an ended SID: the burst writes its
 1134  0000074C  66xx                           bne.s   .out                    ; voice's volume again
 1135  0000074E  6100xxxx                       bsr     yx6_burst_open_old
 1136                                   .out:
 1137  00000752  4E75                           rts
 1138                                   .held:                                  ; E != 0 here: the caller's gate
 1139  00000754  1A00                           move.b  d0,d5
 1140  00000756  CA3C00C0                       and.b   #$C0,d5
 1141  0000075A  BA3C0040                       cmp.b   #$40,d5
 1142  0000075E  67A8                           beq.s   .drum                   ; a held drum code triggers again
 1143  00000760  B22D0001                       cmp.b   SD_TLAST(a5),d1         ; reload only a CHANGED count: a write
 1144  00000764  67xx                           beq.s   .track                  ; meeting the counter at 01 is
 1145  00000766  1B410001                       move.b  d1,SD_TLAST(a5)         ; indeterminate, so do not roll dice
 1146  0000076A  346D0004                       movea.w SD_DATA(a5),a2          ; fifty times a second
 1147  0000076E  1481                           move.b  d1,(a2)
 1148                                   .track:
 1149                                   ; The level-held effects track their parameter every frame: the SID volume
 1150                                   ; and the buzzer shape follow the voice's ring byte, as the reference player
 1151                                   ; re-reads them.
 1152  00000770  2053                           movea.l YX6_CURSOR(a3),a0
 1153  00000772  D1ED0008                       adda.l  SD_PARM(a5),a0
 1154  00000776  1810                           move.b  (a0),d4
 1155  00000778  C83C000F                       and.b   #15,d4
 1156  0000077C  206D000C                       movea.l SD_ISR(a5),a0
 1157  00000780  4A05                           tst.b   d5                      ; $00 SID, $C0 buzzer
 1158  00000782  67xx                           beq.s   .vol
 1159  00000784  11440082                       move.b  d4,ISR_BUZZ_SHAPE(a0)
 1160  00000788  4E75                           rts
 1161                                   .vol:
 1162  0000078A  1144004E                       move.b  d4,ISR_SID_VOL(a0)
 1163  0000078E  4E75                           rts
 1164                                   
 1165                                   ; Opens the burst gate of the voice the OLD code (d4) named, when that code
 1166                                   ; was a SID; anything else leaves the gates alone. Clobbers d2/a0, keeps d4.
 1167                                   yx6_burst_open_old:
 1168  00000790  1404                           move.b  d4,d2
 1169  00000792  C43C00C0                       and.b   #$C0,d2
 1170  00000796  66xx                           bne.s   .not_sid
 1171  00000798  4A04                           tst.b   d4
 1172  0000079A  67xx                           beq.s   .not_sid
 1173  0000079C  1404                           move.b  d4,d2
 1174  0000079E  E80A                           lsr.b   #4,d2
 1175  000007A0  C47C0003                       and.w   #3,d2
 1176  000007A4  5342                           subq.w  #1,d2
 1177  000007A6  3F04                           move.w  d4,-(sp)
 1178  000007A8  7802                           moveq   #2,d4
 1179  000007AA  6100xxxx                       bsr     yx6_burst_gate
 1180  000007AE  381F                           move.w  (sp)+,d4
 1181                                   .not_sid:
 1182  000007B0  4E75                           rts
 1183                                   
 1184                                   ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1185                                   ; yx6_drum_start: read the sample number from the drummed voice's ring byte
 1186                                   ; and silence that byte - it is a number, not a volume - flag the voice for
 1187                                   ; the mixer pass, then arm the drum routine: its select register, its sample
 1188                                   ; address, and the flag-clearing mask, all patched into its own operands.
 1189                                   ; If the other slot holds a SID on this same voice, the drum takes the
 1190                                   ; volume register for itself, as the reference player's drum override does:
 1191                                   ; stop that SID's timer and clear its last code, so the slot retries every
 1192                                   ; frame - suppressed while the flag is up - and restarts the SID by itself
 1193                                   ; the frame after the drum ends.
 1194                                   ; Called from the new-code path AND the held retrigger, which has neither
 1195                                   ; the voice nor the parameter pointer at hand - so it derives its own.
 1196                                   ; in: d0.b = E, a5 = the descriptor, SD_PARM set; out: a0 = the handler;
 1197                                   ; keeps d0/d1/d3/a5.
 1198                                   ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1199                                   yx6_drum_start:
 1200  000007B2  2053                           movea.l YX6_CURSOR(a3),a0
 1201  000007B4  D1ED0008                       adda.l  SD_PARM(a5),a0
 1202  000007B8  6100xxxx                       bsr     yx6_borrow              ; the ring gets this byte back
 1203  000007BC  7800                           moveq   #0,d4
 1204  000007BE  1810                           move.b  (a0),d4                 ; the sample number, validated at pack
 1205  000007C0  4210                           clr.b   (a0)                    ; sanitize: silence, not a number
 1206  000007C2  1A00                           move.b  d0,d5
 1207  000007C4  E80D                           lsr.b   #4,d5
 1208  000007C6  CA7C0003                       and.w   #3,d5
 1209  000007CA  5345                           subq.w  #1,d5                   ; the voice, for flag and select
 1210  000007CC  7401                           moveq   #1,d2
 1211  000007CE  EB2A                           lsl.b   d5,d2
 1212  000007D0  41FAFE3E                       lea     yx6_drums(pc),a0
 1213  000007D4  8510                           or.b    d2,(a0)                 ; the mixer pass forces this voice off
 1214  000007D6  4602                           not.b   d2
 1215  000007D8  226D000C                       movea.l SD_ISR(a5),a1
 1216  000007DC  1342003F                       move.b  d2,ISR_CLEAR(a1)        ; the end tick drops the same flag
 1217  000007E0  286D0010                       movea.l SD_OTHER(a5),a4         ; a SID on this voice in the other
 1218  000007E4  1414                           move.b  (a4),d2                 ; slot: the drum takes the register
 1219  000007E6  C43C00C0                       and.b   #$C0,d2
 1220  000007EA  66xx                           bne.s   .keep
 1221  000007EC  1414                           move.b  (a4),d2
 1222  000007EE  B102                           eor.b   d0,d2
 1223  000007F0  C43C0030                       and.b   #$30,d2
 1224  000007F4  66xx                           bne.s   .keep
 1225  000007F6  306C0002                       movea.w SD_CTRL(a4),a0
 1226  000007FA  021000F0                       andi.b  #$F0,(a0)               ; stop the other slot's SID
 1227  000007FE  4214                           clr.b   (a4)                    ; and let it retry every frame
 1228  00000800  3405                           move.w  d5,d2                   ; the drum owns the volume register
 1229  00000802  3F04                           move.w  d4,-(sp)                ; through its own sanitize now: the
 1230  00000804  7802                           moveq   #2,d4                   ; burst write comes back
 1231  00000806  6100xxxx                       bsr     yx6_burst_gate
 1232  0000080A  381F                           move.w  (sp)+,d4
 1233                                   .keep:
 1234  0000080C  3405                           move.w  d5,d2
 1235  0000080E  5042                           addq.w  #8,d2                   ; the voice's volume register
 1236  00000810  13420003                       move.b  d2,ISR_DRUM_SEL(a1)     ; the tick's select immediate
 1237  00000814  D844                           add.w   d4,d4                   ; a drum table entry is six bytes:
 1238  00000816  3404                           move.w  d4,d2                   ; 6n = 2n + 4n
 1239  00000818  D844                           add.w   d4,d4
 1240  0000081A  D842                           add.w   d2,d4
 1241  0000081C  246B0014                       movea.l YX6_FILE(a3),a2
 1242  00000820  204A                           movea.l a2,a0
 1243  00000822  D1E8001C                       adda.l  YH_DRUMS(a0),a0
 1244  00000826  24304000                       move.l  0(a0,d4.w),d2           ; the sample's offset in the file
 1245  0000082A  D48A                           add.l   a2,d2
 1246  0000082C  23420008                       move.l  d2,ISR_DRUM_PTR(a1)     ; the tick reads through its operand
 1247  00000830  2049                           movea.l a1,a0                   ; the handler is the block base
 1248  00000832  4E75                           rts
 1249                                   
 1250                                   ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1251                                   ; yx6_burst_gate: while a SID owns a voice, the burst must NOT write that
 1252                                   ; voice's volume register - the write lands mid-phase and forces the loud
 1253                                   ; half back for up to half a square period, an audible tick at bass rates.
 1254                                   ; Both references agree: ST-Sound's per-sample SID overrides the register
 1255                                   ; write, maxYMiser's frame code skips SID channels outright. The gate is
 1256                                   ; one SMC word: the volume write's destination displacement, 2 for the PSG
 1257                                   ; data register, 0 to land harmlessly on the select register instead - the
 1258                                   ; next macro's select overrides it before anything is written.
 1259                                   ; in: d2.w = the voice 0..2, d4.w = 2 to open or 0 to mute; clobbers d2/a0.
 1260                                   ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1261                                   yx6_burst_gate:
 1262  00000834  C4FC000A                       mulu.w  #10,d2                  ; a YX6_WRITE is ten bytes; R8's
 1263  00000838  41FAFB5C                       lea     yx6_wB+8(pc),a0         ; destination word sits at +8
 1264  0000083C  31842000                       move.w  d4,0(a0,d2.w)
 1265  00000840  4E75                           rts
 1266                                   
 1267                                   ; Records a ring byte about to be edited, for the restore after the burst.
 1268                                   ; in: a0 = the byte; clobbers d2.
 1269                                   yx6_borrow:
 1270  00000842  7400                           moveq   #0,d2
 1271  00000844  142B0036                       move.b  YX6_PATCHES(a3),d2
 1272  00000848  522B0036                       addq.b  #1,YX6_PATCHES(a3)
 1273  0000084C  17902037                       move.b  (a0),YX6_PATCH_VAL(a3,d2.w)
 1274  00000850  D442                           add.w   d2,d2
 1275  00000852  D442                           add.w   d2,d2
 1276  00000854  2788203A                       move.l  a0,YX6_PATCH_ADDR(a3,d2.w)
 1277  00000858  4E75                           rts
    3                                           include "ST4_wrap.S"
    1                                   ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    2                                   ; ST4_wrap.S - ST4: counted-wrap ring decompressor for the Atari ST (68000)
    3                                   ;
    4                                   ; By Robbert van Dalen, based on ZX1 v1.5 by Einar Saukas and on ST1_wrap.S,
    5                                   ; which this follows instruction for instruction wherever the format allows.
    6                                   ; Written by Claude (Anthropic's Claude Code) under Robbert's direction. Use it
    7                                   ; freely, including commercially, as long as your documentation says you used
    8                                   ; ZX1 through ST4 (see LICENSE).
    9                                   ;
   10                                   ; ST4_wrap is ST1_wrap with ST4's three streams and units: the literal payload
   11                                   ; comes from stream B in a2 and the offsets from stream C in a4 (bytes) or D in
   12                                   ; a5 (words), lengths and budgets count units of ST4_UNIT bytes, and the copy
   13                                   ; moves a unit at a time. With the offsets gone, stream A holds nothing but
   14                                   ; bits, so the queue refills a word at a time. Set the unit size before
   15                                   ; including:
   16                                   ;
   17                                   ;       ST4_UNIT    equ     4
   18                                   ;               include "ST4_wrap.S"
   19                                   ;
   20                                   ; !!! LIKE ST1_wrap, THIS HAS NO DONE STATE !!!
   21                                   ; The caller must know the exact output size O in units, make exactly
   22                                   ; T = ceil(O/C) decode calls, and never call again.
   23                                   ;
   24                                   ; The ring is N BYTES and must be a whole number of units; the caller resets a1
   25                                   ; to the ring start after every F = N/(C*UNIT) calls. Budgets and remaining
   26                                   ; counts are units; offsets, positions and N are bytes - which is what keeps
   27                                   ; the wrap arithmetic identical to ST1_wrap's.
   28                                   ;
   29                                   ; The copy engine runs two ladders. Match segments of at most sixteen units -
   30                                   ; measured to be four of every five segments a small-budget streamer makes,
   31                                   ; with the whole-chunk segment the most common of all - take a counter-free
   32                                   ; ladder that falls straight into the match epilogue: no full-pass shift, no
   33                                   ; loop counter, no post-copy kind test. Literals and longer segments take the
   34                                   ; classic counted ladder. And there is no first-call test: ST4_init seeds the
   35                                   ; bit queue with one virtual 0 flag ($4000), so a fresh state decodes itself
   36                                   ; through a zero-unit match segment and reads that flag as "begin literals"
   37                                   ; exactly where the old entry branch went.
   38                                   ;
   39                                   ; The container is ST4.S's: twenty bytes of header - signature, O, and where
   40                                   ; streams B, C and D begin - then A, B, C and D in order. See ST4.S for the
   41                                   ; eight instructions that open one.
   42                                   ;
   43                                   ; THE STATE IS YOUR REGISTERS:
   44                                   ;
   45                                   ;   a0.l  position in stream A          a1.l  write pointer
   46                                   ;   a2.l  position in stream B          a4.l  stream C, the byte offsets
   47                                   ;   a5.l  stream D, the word offsets    d0.w  bit queue ($8000 empty)
   48                                   ;   d1.l  -ring_start.low : units remaining
   49                                   ;   d2.l  N : signed byte offset (+lastOffset*UNIT literals, - during a match)
   50                                   ;
   51                                   ;   resume in    d3.w   this call's budget in UNITS
   52                                   ;   CLOBBERED    d3.w d4.l d5.l a3.l
   53                                   ;   untouched    d6 d7 a6
   54                                   ;
   55                                   ; ASSUMPTIONS (unchecked; violating them is undefined behaviour):
   56                                   ;
   57                                   ;   1. THE RING SIZE N IS A MULTIPLE OF ST4_UNIT, and the ring starts on a unit
   58                                   ;      boundary - as does the destination. A wide move would fault on an odd
   59                                   ;      address, and the caller's wrap has to land on a unit
   60                                   ;   2. N mod (C * ST4_UNIT) = 0, so F = N/(C*UNIT) calls fill the ring exactly
   61                                   ;   3. no offset exceeds 32512 BYTES - the packer clamps -m to that
   62                                   ;   4. no operation is longer than 65535 units - compress with -l65535
   63                                   ;   5. the caller knows O and makes exactly T = ceil(O/C) calls, as ST1_wrap
   64                                   ;   6. streams A and D start on even addresses, and A holds an even number of
   65                                   ;      bytes: the bit queue is refilled with a move.w, and so is a word offset
   66                                   ;
   67                                   ; Compress with st4 -k<UNIT> -m<ring/UNIT> -l65535.
   68                                   ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   69                                   
   70                                   ; The asset's signature for this build: 'S4', format version 4, and the unit
   71                                   ; size this decoder was assembled for. One cmp.l against a file's first long
   72                                   ; proves all three at once.
   73           =53340402               ST4_SIGNATURE   equ     $53340400+ST4_UNIT
   74                                   
   75                                   ; One unit copied, at the width this build was made for.
   76                                           .macro ST4_MOVE
   77                                 .         .if ST4_UNIT=4
   78                                 .         move.l  (a3)+,(a1)+
   79                                 .         .endif
   80                                 .         .if ST4_UNIT=2
   81                                 .         move.w  (a3)+,(a1)+
   82                                 .         .endif
   83                                 .         .if ST4_UNIT=1
   84                                 .         move.b  (a3)+,(a1)+
   85                                 .         .endif
   86                                 .         .endm
   87                                   
   88                                   ; Jump table: bra.w keeps 4-byte slots (base+0/4). A counted ring has no
   89                                   ; one-shot entry; the caller drains it through ST4_resume.
   90  0000085A  6000xxxx                               bra.w   ST4_init
   91  0000085E  6000xxxx                               bra.w   ST4_resume
   92                                   
   93                                   ; ST4_init: a0 = A, a1 = first ring byte, a2 = B, a4 = C, a5 = D,
   94                                   ; d3.w = N.
   95                                   ; Packs -ring_start.low/N above the common state; writes no memory.
   96                                   ; Java: the Decompressor constructor / reset().
   97                                   ST4_init:
   98  00000862  7200                           moveq   #0,d1
   99  00000864  9249                           sub.w   a1,d1                   ; -ring_start.low, exact modulo 65536
  100  00000866  4841                           swap    d1                      ; pack it high; remaining = 0
  101                                   ; The initial offset has to land in the low word after the swap, so it starts
  102                                   ; in the high half. moveq would put -1 there whatever the unit size, which is
  103                                   ; a byte short at k=2 and three at k=4 - and only shows up on a stream whose
  104                                   ; first match reuses INITIAL_OFFSET.
  105  00000868  243CFFFE0000                   move.l  #(-ST4_UNIT)<<16,d2
  106  0000086E  3403                           move.w  d3,d2                   ; N lives below it for now
  107  00000870  4842                           swap    d2                      ; N above, -INITIAL_OFFSET*UNIT below
  108                                   ; One virtual 0 flag, then the empty sentinel. The first resume call runs a
  109                                   ; zero-unit match segment through the normal path - remaining is zero, so
  110                                   ; n = 0 and the ladder is entered at its end - and lands in match_transition,
